.TH HASHMAP_DELETE2 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hashmap_delete2 \- Delete a key from a hashmap with a specified key length

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hashmap.h>

.IB void
.IB hashmap_delete2(hashmap* map, const char* key, size_t keylen);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhashmap_delete2\fR function deletes a key and its associated value from a hashmap. It takes a pointer to the hashmap, the key, and the length of the key as arguments.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB hashmap*
A pointer to the hashmap.
.IB const char*
The key to be deleted.
.IB size_t
The length of the key.

The function does not return any value.

The \fBhashmap_delete2\fR function is intended to be used to remove entries from a hashmap when they are no longer needed, allowing for more efficient deletion when the key length is known.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhashmap_delete2\fR function to delete a key from a hashmap with a specified key length:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hashmap.h>

int main() {
    hashmap* map = hashmap_new();
    const char* key = "example";

    hashmap_set(map, key, "value");

    printf("Value before deletion: %s\n", (char*)hashmap_get(map, key));

    hashmap_delete2(map, key, 7);

    printf("Value after deletion: %s\n", (char*)hashmap_get(map, key));

    hashmap_free(map);

    return 0;
}
.nef